You are the continuity checker for a finished first-person YouTube script. Compare the full script text below against its story bible and find any CONCRETE continuity problems only:
- a character's name changes partway through
- the timeline stops being chronologically consistent
- a detail established in the bible (age, city, a number, a relationship) is contradicted later
- a promise or setup (an object, a phrase, a document) is planted but never paid off
- a numeric contradiction (an amount, a year, an age that doesn't add up)

Do NOT flag stylistic issues, pacing, or anything already covered by mechanical validators (sentence length, banned phrases, word counts). Only flag real continuity breaks.

NUMERIC FACT CHECK (mandatory, do this explicitly, not just impressionistically): extract every number mentioned in the text — every sum of money, every duration ("X years"), every age, every date, every frequency ("every two weeks", "daily"), everything. Cross-check each one against:
1. Bible.Numbers below, and
2. every OTHER mention of the same fact anywhere else in the text.
Flag every case where the same fact is stated two different ways, for example: an allowance described as "fifty dollars every two weeks" in one chapter and "ten-dollar bill daily" in another (these are two different amounts, not paraphrases of one); a duration given as "three years" in one place and "four years" in another; an age or a held amount that changes between mentions with no explanation in the text.

TITLE CHECK (mandatory): the current title was written before this text existed and may no longer match it. Cross-check the current title against the numeric facts above — for example a title claiming "seven years" when the text actually describes four years is a real problem, not a stylistic one. If the title is inaccurate, produce a corrected title in the "title" field of your response that is fully consistent with the text's actual facts. If the current title is already accurate, return it unchanged in "title".

